    <div class="mgdb-hero-body">
      <h1 id="cp-title">Controlled pollination of maize</h1>
      <p class="mgdb-hero-description">A field protocol for researchers and their assistants who are new to making controlled crosses in maize, <em>Zea mays</em> L. It covers the whole operation in order &mdash; bagging the ear shoot, cutting back, collecting pollen, pollinating, and the precautions that decide whether any of it works &mdash; with $(clip_count) short demonstrations filmed at the University of Missouri-Columbia.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <nav class="mgdb-section-tabs" aria-label="Sections on this page">
    <a href="#cp-overview" class="is-current" aria-current="true">Overview</a>
    <a href="#cp-bagging">1. Bagging</a>
    <a href="#cp-cutting">2. Cutting back</a>
    <a href="#cp-pollen">3. Pollen</a>
    <a href="#cp-pollination">4. Pollination</a>
    <a href="#cp-precautions">5. Precautions</a>
    <a href="#cp-supplies">6. Supplies</a>
    <a href="#cp-references">7. References</a>
    <a href="#cp-related">Related resources</a>
  </nav>

  <section id="cp-overview" aria-labelledby="cp-overview-title">
    <div class="mgdb-section-heading">
      <div><h2 id="cp-overview-title">Overview</h2></div>
    </div>

    <div class="cp-prose">
      <p>Controlled pollination is how a maize geneticist decides which plant fertilizes which. Every cross, every self, every mapping population depends on the silks receiving the pollen you chose and no other. That is entirely a matter of technique in the field, and it is unforgiving: an ear shoot bagged a day late, or a tassel bag left on in the rain, loses the cross.</p>

      <p>The operation breaks into the steps below, in the order you carry them out. Each carries the field demonstrations that illustrate it &mdash; $(clip_count) clips, $(clip_runtime) in all &mdash; and none of them loads from Vimeo until you press play.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="cp-source">
      <h3>Where this comes from</h3>
      <p>The text is M. G. Neuffer's, from Chapter 3, &ldquo;Growing Maize for Genetic Purposes,&rdquo; in William F. Sheridan (ed.&#41;, <em>Maize for Biological Research</em>, Plant Molecular Biology Association, 1982 (ISBN 0-9608758-0-8&#41;, together with personal communications from Susan Melia-Hancock of the University of Missouri-Columbia, a very experienced maize pollinator. The dating method in step 5.3 is hers, and originates with Dr. E. H. Coe Jr.</p>
      <p>The video clips show the methods used by many of the maize researchers at the University of Missouri-Columbia.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="cp-bagging" aria-labelledby="cp-bagging-title">
    <div class="mgdb-section-heading">
      <div><h2 id="cp-bagging-title">1. Bagging the ear shoot</h2></div>
    </div>

    <div class="cp-prose">
      <p>Bagging is a fundamental requirement and yet, of all the pollinating operations, it is the most difficult to do properly. The ear shoots (lateral branches&#41; must be covered <strong>before the silks (styles&#41; emerge</strong>, to protect them from being contaminated by pollen until the desired pollen can be applied.</p>

      <p>The bag used (Lawson 217&#41; measures 2 &times; 1 &times; 7 inches (5 &times; 2.5 &times; 18 cm&#41; and is made of semi-transparent treated 36 lb wet-strength paper with waterproof glue. The daily shoot-bagging operation should begin when the first tassels appear. At that time the tip of the first ear shoot may be visible in the axil of the 6th or 7th leaf down from the top of the plant. Cover it by placing the bag over the tip of the shoot with the longer lip of the bag next to the stem (culm&#41;, so that the shorter lip slides between the tip of the ear shoot and the adjacent leaf sheath. Pull the edges of the bag around to conform to the shape of the culm, and give the bag a sharp downward pull to attach it firmly between those tissues. Examine the plants every day to cover new ears as they emerge; at the same time, bags already on the plants may be pulled down again to make them more secure.</p>

      <p>If you have vigorous plants with large ears or leafy tips, they can grow through the end of closely tucked bags, especially in rainy weather. The bags may need to be loosened, the leafy part trimmed, or soaked bags replaced. Other, less vigorous plants can produce silks while the ear is still completely hidden in the sheath. If the plant is shedding pollen and no ear shoot has been bagged, check for the telltale bulging of the leaf sheath beneath the leaf where an ear is expected; carefully pull the sheath back slightly and bend the leaf down, or rip the leaf off at the collar, so the shoot bag will fit securely over the ear and the sheath around it.</p>

      <p>Bagging too early is not very satisfactory, because the tissues will not hold the bag firmly in place. Bagging too late risks exposure of silks and contamination. <strong>Ears should not be bagged after silks appear.</strong> Complete honesty is required of all workers in this regard, or contamination will get completely out of hand.</p>

      <p>Some workers use a larger bag, 2&frac12; &times; 1 &times; 8&frac12; inches (6.4 &times; 2.5 &times; 22 cm&#41; (Lawson 218&#41;, bend back the 7th leaf, cover the whole auricle and ligule area at the top of the leaf sheath, and staple the bag around the culm even before the first shoot appears. This allows one-trip shoot bagging, but sometimes the wrong leaf axil is taken and the shoot comes out elsewhere and is exposed. In any shoot-bagging practice, take care in handling the plant so that the support of the leaf sheath to the culm of the next node is not weakened; otherwise the top of the plant will break out and the whole plant is lost.</p>

      <p>Under natural conditions receptive silks appear over a 4&ndash;5 day period and are pollinated by the pollen shed on each of those days, giving a full set. In hand pollination the pollen is applied only once, in a very short period. The result would be a partially filled ear unless additional steps are taken; the standard practice to overcome this is <a href="#cp-cutting">cutting back</a>.</p>
    </div>

    $(clips_1)
  </section>

  <section id="cp-cutting" aria-labelledby="cp-cutting-title">
    <div class="mgdb-section-heading">
      <div><h2 id="cp-cutting-title">2. Cutting back an ear</h2></div>
    </div>

    <div class="cp-prose">
      <p>Cutting back consists of cutting off, with a knife, the tip of the husks and silks on an ear having the first day's silks visible, and then re-covering the ear to prevent exposure. Make the cut squarely and cleanly across the ear and as far down the husks as possible, without cutting off the tip of the cob inside. Contamination is avoided because the wet ends of freshly cut silks are not receptive to stray pollen grains that may fall on them during the operation. Mark the shoot bag covering the cut silks by folding the corner, to record which ears were cut back.</p>

      <p>The next day the silks will have grown into a thick brush, all the same length and all ready for pollination. Pollen applied then will reach every silk and give a full set. Pollinated silks stop growing in an hour or so and become darker in color. Under normal conditions silks are receptive for about 3 days; after that the chance of successful pollination diminishes rapidly.</p>

      <p>Some inbred lines have very tight husk tips that protect them from bird damage. These husks must be cut back before silks can be seen, since they will be past the receptive stage before the mass of silks is large enough to push up through the husks.</p>

      <p>Note that unpollinated silks grow continually during this period, to a length of 6&ndash;8 inches (15&ndash;20 cm&#41; or more. Failure of unpollinated silks to re-grow, or irregular growth, indicates that the silks were already past the most receptive stage and that conditions will not be good for pollen germination.</p>
    </div>

    $(clips_2)
  </section>

  <section id="cp-pollen" aria-labelledby="cp-pollen-title">
    <div class="mgdb-section-heading">
      <div><h2 id="cp-pollen-title">3. Pollen production</h2></div>
    </div>

    <div class="cp-prose">
      <p>Pollen is shed from mature anthers beginning on the upper third of the main spike of the tassel, usually 1 or 2 days before silks appear on the ear shoots. The shedding is in daily increments that spread downward to the tassel branches toward the base and upward towards the tip of the main spike, moving along the tassel in two waves about 2 days apart: the first from the anthers of the upper floret of each spikelet, the second from the lower floret. A healthy tassel should shed pollen for about 1 week.</p>

      <p>On a warm sunny day fresh anthers begin to extrude from the florets by elongation of the filaments at about 7 a.m. (07:00&#41;. These anthers open (dehisce&#41; about 30 minutes later, and pollen falls out and is scattered by air currents. As the morning progresses additional anthers appear until about 10 a.m.; after that, under these conditions, no new anthers appear until the next morning. If the weather is cool or cloudy and humid, extrusion is delayed until later, sometimes until evening. Some workers report successful pollinations made in the evening; the author finds this undependable in most conditions and impossible in others. <strong>As long as the humidity is near 100% the anthers will not open.</strong> If the weather is hot and dry the pollen-shedding process proceeds more rapidly.</p>
    </div>

    $(clips_3)
  </section>

  <section id="cp-pollination" aria-labelledby="cp-pollination-title">
    <div class="mgdb-section-heading">
      <div><h2 id="cp-pollination-title">4. Pollination</h2></div>
    </div>

    <div class="cp-step" id="cp-4-1">
      <h3>4.1 Collecting pollen</h3>
      <div class="cp-prose">
        <p>To make controlled pollinations it is necessary to collect viable pollen. Cover shedding tassels, before the pollen is shed, with a brown paper tassel bag (Lawson No. 402, &ldquo;Showerproof'd&rdquo;&#41;, then collect and carry the pollen to the desired protected silks. The bag is also the best place for recording information about the pollen parent, any traits seen in the ear parent, the pollination date and so on. Information about a pollen parent from which a single pollination is to be made can be written immediately after the tassel has been covered. If more than one female is to be pollinated from a single tassel, keep a list of bags to write, or write up additional bags as they are put on and keep them for the next day's pollinations.</p>

        <p>If the plants to be used as male parents are sturdy and heavy winds are not forecast, the best time to cover the tassels is in the evening, or after pollen has shed on the previous day. If the plants are weak or heavy winds are expected, bag the tassels after the dew is off but before shedding has ended on the morning of the desired pollinations. Moisture from dew trapped in the tassel bag raises the humidity inside and will prevent the anthers from opening.</p>
      </div>

      $(clips_4_1)
    </div>

    <div class="cp-step" id="cp-4-2">
      <h3>4.2 Tassel bagging, and the pollination itself</h3>
      <div class="cp-prose">
        <p>Cover the tassel with the brown paper tassel bag, keeping the tassel as flat (horizontal&#41; as possible. Pull the bag down past the first flag leaf, which may be removed, then fold the base of the bag firmly around the sheath and stem of the tassel and secure it with a regular paperclip. The bag can be stapled in place, but the time saved is often wasted removing the staple the next day, when speed is essential.</p>

        <p>If tassels were bagged the day before, pollination can begin as soon as nearby unbagged tassels are seen to be shedding &mdash; bees determine this proper time very well and can be a good indicator. If tassels are bagged in the morning, allow sufficient time, at least half an hour, for all stray pollen grains to die and for anthers with fresh pollen to emerge inside the bag. The tassel bags commonly used are water resistant but not waterproof; if they have been soaked by heavy rain during the night and will not dry before pollen shed begins, replace them with dry bags rather than lose a day of shed.</p>

        <p>To collect the pollen, carefully bend the plant so that the open end of the bag is higher than the closed end. Remove the paperclip and shake the bag and tassel sharply. Withdraw the tassel, taking care not to let the open end of the bag fall low enough for the pollen to spill. The pollen can be carried this way to make a self pollination, or to cross onto the silks of one or two nearby plants. <strong>Do not depend on pollen remaining viable in the removed tassel bag for more than 10&ndash;30 minutes</strong>, depending on conditions.</p>

        <p>There are as many variations in this procedure as there are principal investigators, but the objective is always the same: apply the chosen pollen to the chosen silks and minimize contamination. One standard procedure is to carry the tassel bag, open end folded, to the plant whose silks are to be pollinated. Place the stalk and tassel of the female parent under your arm, bending the plant so that its tassel is at your back and the ear immediately in front &mdash; this helps protect the ear from pollen falling from its own tassel. Raise the shoot bag slightly, tear off the top, and squeeze it so the bag forms a chimney above the silks, which protects them from pollen carried by air currents from the side. Open the tassel bag so the pollen can be poured out in small amounts, dust it on the silks, and quickly fold over the torn end of the shoot bag. Then pull the tassel bag over the pollinated ear so the long lip slides between the culm and the ear and the short lip hangs loosely on the outside; pull the two edges on the long-lip side around the stalk and staple them together on the side opposite the ear, so the bag is secured to the stalk and the ear is free to enlarge without disrupting it. If further pollinations are to be made from the same male, fold the top of the pollen bag closed and move to the next female.</p>
      </div>

      $(clips_4_2)
    </div>

    <div class="cp-step" id="cp-4-3">
      <h3>4.3 Many pollinations from one tassel, using a glassine bag</h3>
      <div class="cp-prose">
        <p>If a large number of pollinations are to be made in a single day from one pollen source &mdash; as many as 100 pollinations may be made from one tassel &mdash; the best procedure is as follows. Prepare a glassine bag, 2 &times; 7&frac12; inches (5 &times; 19 cm&#41;, by making a Z-shaped fold about halfway up its length. Pour in the contents of the tassel bag and sift the pollen past the first fold into the bottom fold, keeping the anthers in the top, where they can be discarded. Turn the bag on its side and tear off the upper of the two bottom corners.</p>

        <p>Carry the pollen in this bag to the waiting silks, where it can be sifted sparingly through the torn corner into the top of the torn shoot bag covering each ear. Fold the shoot bag to protect the pollinated silks and move rapidly to the next ear. <strong>Speed is essential here</strong>, because the pollen will remain viable only a short time. Fresh pollen appears light yellow and flows freely from the glassine bag; if it becomes deeper yellow or clumps together, it is no longer viable &mdash; collectively speaking, though some viable grains may remain among the majority of unviable ones.</p>

        <p>A pre-written tassel bag can be left between the shoot bag and the culm, to be secured later. The pre-written information should comprise the identity of the host plant bearing the ear, sometimes called the female plant, and the identity of the pollen-producing plant, sometimes called the male; notes about the pollination and the date may be written there too. After all pollinations from one source are complete, there is time to go back, place the tassel bags over the ears and staple them.</p>
      </div>

      $(clips_4_3)
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="cp-precautions" aria-labelledby="cp-precautions-title">
    <div class="mgdb-section-heading">
      <div><h2 id="cp-precautions-title">5. Some precautions</h2></div>
    </div>

    <div class="cp-step" id="cp-5-1">
      <h3>5.1 Contamination</h3>
      <div class="cp-prose">
        <p>During the shedding period pollen will be airborne on air currents. It will also be on leaves, on the pollinator's clothing and on their hands. Most of it will already be unviable, but enough will be viable to make contamination a serious problem. Take precautions to keep receptive silks covered at all times.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="cp-step" id="cp-5-2">
      <h3>5.2 Pollen viability</h3>
      <div class="cp-prose">
        <p>The life of a pollen grain is usually less than 20 minutes after it leaves the anther &mdash; less in hot dry weather, and much more in cool humid weather. Ways to extend pollen viability considerably are known, but the author has not found them reliable; they require special knowledge and handling.</p>

        <p>There is little hope of fertilization if the temperature goes much above 95&deg;F (35&deg;C&#41; during the shedding period or for several hours afterwards. Conversely, pollen may appear normal but will not grow on the silks unless the temperature during pollination and for some hours after goes above 55&deg;F (13&deg;C&#41;.</p>

        <p>Pollen is especially sensitive to drying. Any time pollen grains change color or begin to collapse, irreversible degeneration may already have occurred. High humidity has different but equally important consequences: anthers will not open in high humidity, trapping the pollen within. That does not kill the pollen immediately, but it makes spreading it mechanically difficult and causes premature loss of viability. Moisture on silks, in bags, or in other containers for carrying pollen causes immediate loss of viability. Pollen is promptly killed when immersed in water and most other liquids; known exceptions are paraffin oil and properly buffered aqueous solutions. See Poehlman &amp; Sleper (1995&#41;, in the references below, for more on pollination practices.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="cp-step" id="cp-5-3">
      <h3>5.3 Dating tassel bags</h3>
      <div class="cp-prose">
        <p>For some projects it is convenient to have the date of pollination recorded on every tassel bag, but writing on each one is time consuming. With a little practice, hundreds of tassel bags can be dated in a few minutes.</p>

        <p>With the closed end of a tassel bag as the top, make a series of five evenly spaced marks across the top of a bag with a permanent marker or heavy pencil, starting at the left margin and finishing at the right. The third mark will be in the center. Under the leftmost mark write <strong>16</strong>, then <strong>8</strong>, center <strong>4</strong>, then <strong>2</strong>, and under the rightmost mark <strong>1</strong>. That is your key and template: every date from the 1st to the 31st is the sum of the marked positions. The 1st of the month is the rightmost mark alone; the 31st is all five (16 + 8 + 4 + 2 + 1&#41;&#59; the 17th is the leftmost and rightmost (16 + 1&#41;.</p>

        <p>Make a template on a tassel bag showing the desired date of pollination. Fan a stack of 25 to 50 tassel bags so the closed ends are laid out like stair steps, place the template on top of the sloped stack, and make the combination of marks that adds up to the date down the entire stack. To tell months apart, use different colors of marker.</p>
      </div>

      <p class="cp-attribution">Contributed by Susan Melia-Hancock, Maize Mapping Project, Department of Agronomy, University of Missouri-Columbia. The method originates with Dr. E. H. Coe Jr.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

